			</div>
			<!-- TELEMETRY STRIP - the hero's standing claims in the voice every
			     later chapter speaks. The chips carry the three facts the sentence
			     below states plus the one the lead states, and the sentence stays:
			     a hero that ends on prose reads as a publication, a hero that ends
			     on a chip row reads as a spec sheet. Both, in that order, so the
			     reader meets the claim twice and never has to choose a register. -->
			<ul class="chips mt-10 justify-center">
				<li class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--bone">One binary</li>
				<li class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--teal">Zero dependencies</li>
				<li class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--canary">Millions of tokens saved</li>
				<li class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--crimson">One retrieval away</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="mt-8 font-mono text-base font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-zinc-dust">
				One binary. Zero dependencies. Millions of tokens saved.
			</p>
		</header>

		<!-- 02 // THE DIFFERENCE - the product's whole argument, and the second
		     thing on the page. ZineSplit owns the plate and the two panes; above
		     it there is one claim and one introduction, and nothing else, because
		     a demonstration that needs to be explained first is not a
		     demonstration. The instructions did not go anywhere: they are the
		     fold under the plate, verbatim, for the reader the plate has already
		     convinced. `Cycle` above holds the scripts.
		     The section is the entrance root: it reveals on first view and its
		     plate wipes once. The left pane is a terminal that never stops - five
		     commands in a cycle, each streaming its real output on its own rhythm
		     - and the right pane is recomputed from the stream so far, so the
		     marker is never a lie about a flood that has not finished arriving. -->
		<section class="band relative w-full" data-reveal>
			<p class="section-index">02 // THE DIFFERENCE // THE PAIR // STREAM + PREVIEW</p>
			<h2 class="type-section mb-6 text-bone-white">
				The agent spends its budget reading noise
			</h2>
			<div class="prose-flow max-w-[62ch]">
				<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
					Every file read, build, code search, or browser open floods the agent's context
					with raw output - compilation logs, accessibility trees, JSON blobs.
				</p>
				<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
					The agent spends its budget reading noise instead of reasoning.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="mt-12">
				<ZineSplit Run={Cycle} Label="ONE LINE, ONE MARKER" Id="hero-run" />
			</div>
			<!-- The one visible caption. It belongs to the plate - it names what
			     the two panes are, so a reader who scrolled straight to the image
			     knows what they are looking at without opening anything. -->
			<p class="figure-caption mt-6">
				The flood arrives one line at a time; the line the agent would have read instead is
				recomputed as each line lands.
			</p>
			<div class="mt-10 max-w-[62ch]">
				<ZineFold Tag="METHOD" Label="How the demonstration works" Body={MethodFold} />
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- BEAT 03 // MECHANISM - below the pair, because the pair is the argument
		     and this is the machinery behind it. The tape that used to stamp this
		     section is the quiet kicker now: `PIPELINE // 4-STAGE ABSORPTION` is
		     a plate label, and a plate label that is also a stamped tape is two
		     things claiming to introduce one section.
		     ONE EXPRESSIVE ASSEMBLY: the stage grid sits inside a single tilted
		     crimson plate, and the four cards inside it stay at the quiet tier -
		     hairline rules, no per-card tilt, no per-card shadow, no tape -
		     because four emphasised cards is four sections pretending to be one.

		     EMPHASIS AND IDENTITY ARE DIFFERENT AXES. Emphasis is the volume
		     knob (shadow, tilt, tape, fill, rotation) and it is turned down
		     here. Identity is the information (accent, stage letter, figure,
		     chip label) and it is not part of the knob: a reader has to be able
		     to tell stage 03 from stage 01 at a glance, and to match it to the
		     same stage on the case study. Flattening the accents to one would
		     have lowered identity, not emphasis. Each card therefore carries
		     its own accent through the left edge, the corner stamp, the
		     heading, the rule and the chip - and only those; the figure and
		     the description stay bone so the reading text never competes. -->
		<section class="band relative w-full" data-reveal>
			<p class="section-index">03 // MECHANISM // PIPELINE // 4-STAGE ABSORPTION</p>
			<h2 class="type-section mb-4 text-bone-white">How it works</h2>
			<p class="type-sub mb-6 text-bone-newsprint">Four steps, one decision</p>
			<div class="prose-flow max-w-2xl">
				<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
					A tool result is appended to the model's context the moment the tool returns, so
					a build log the agent never needed is read in full, and paid for in full.
					Aphrodite intercepts that output before it reaches the LLM and replaces it with
					a compact, structured preview - because the agent's reasoning budget is the
					context budget, and raw noise spends both.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="mt-10 max-w-[62ch]">
				<ZineFold Tag="GUIDE" Label="How to read the four stages" Body={GridFold} />
			</div>
			<!-- The section's one expressive assembly. BENTO GRAMMAR: the four
			     stages are not four equal boxes - 01 and 04 take 45% more width
			     than 02 and 03, because those are the two whose figure needs the
			     room (`40 - 123 ns`, `aphrodite_retrieve`) and because a cluster
			     of four identical tiles reads as a list, not as a mechanism with
			     a shape. Weighted TRACKS rather than column spans: a span of
			     2-of-6 put the narrow cards at 163px and wrapped every sentence
			     in them; 1.45fr against 1fr gives the same two-size read at 308
			     against 212 and keeps the cards readable. The grid keeps its 2x2
			     shape through the tablet range and only splits into weighted
			     tracks at xl, where each track has room to mean something.
			     The plate is inset (`mx-3`/`sm:mx-4`) because a tilted plate's
			     BOUNDING box is wider than its layout box: at -1.2deg a 500px-tall
			     plate adds ~10px per side, and `main > section` clips that overflow,
			     so a full-bleed plate would have its corners and its shadow plane
			     shaved off. -->
			<div
				class="grunge grunge--bone emphasis-quiet mx-3 mt-12 p-5 sm:mx-4 sm:p-8 lg:p-10"
				data-grunge
			>
				<div
					class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4"
					data-enter="180"
				>
					<!-- Stage 01 // CLASSIFY - teal: the technical voice, the type
					     that names the content. -->
					<div class="emphasis-quiet stage-card stage-card--a flex flex-col p-5">
						<div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
							<h3 class="type-meta font-bold stage-ink">01 // CLASSIFY</h3>
							<span class="type-meta stage-ink">STAGE // A</span>
						</div>
						<div class="metric mt-4 mb-4">40 - 123 ns</div>
						<div class="stage-rule mt-auto border-t pt-3">
							<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
								Type-aware classifier names the content.
							</p>
							<p class="type-meta mt-2 stage-ink">SIMD BYTE</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<!-- Stage 02 // ENRICH & PREVIEW - canary: the result the reader
					     acts on. -->
					<div class="emphasis-quiet stage-card stage-card--b flex flex-col p-5">
						<div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
							<h3 class="type-meta font-bold stage-ink">
								02 // ENRICH &amp; PREVIEW
							</h3>
							<span class="type-meta stage-ink">STAGE // B</span>
						</div>
						<div class="metric mt-4 mb-4">~1 ms</div>
						<div class="stage-rule mt-auto border-t pt-3">
							<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
								Enriched, type-aware preview rendering.
							</p>
							<p class="type-meta mt-2 stage-ink">TYPE-AWARE</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<!-- Stage 03 // BLAKE3 STORE - blood: storage, and the marker it
					     hands back. -->
					<div class="emphasis-quiet stage-card stage-card--c flex flex-col p-5">
						<div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
							<h3 class="type-meta font-bold stage-ink">03 // BLAKE3 STORE</h3>
							<span class="type-meta stage-ink">STAGE // C</span>
						</div>
						<div class="metric mt-4 mb-4">SQLite</div>
						<div class="stage-rule mt-auto border-t pt-3">
							<p class="type-body ccr-marker text-bone-newsprint">
								CCR marker: &lt;&lt;&lt;CCR:hash|type|size&gt;&gt;&gt;
							</p>
							<p class="type-meta mt-2 stage-ink">DUAL TIER</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<!-- Stage 04 // AGENT DECIDES - bone: the one step that is the
					     agent's own choice, so it carries the reading voice's colour
					     rather than an engine accent. -->
					<div class="emphasis-quiet stage-card stage-card--d flex flex-col p-5">
						<div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
							<h3 class="type-meta font-bold stage-ink">04 // AGENT DECIDES</h3>
							<span class="type-meta stage-ink">STAGE // D</span>
						</div>
						<div class="metric mt-4 mb-4">
							aphrodite_<wbr />retrieve
						</div>
						<div class="stage-rule mt-auto border-t pt-3">
							<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
								Full content only when needed.
							</p>
							<p class="type-meta mt-2 stage-ink">ON DEMAND</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- BEAT 04 // EVIDENCE - the chapter break, and the only surface change on
		     the page. Dark wall, one bone clipping: a result pasted onto the
		     publication rather than another panel in it, which is what makes the
		     subject change legible without a single extra word.
		     ONE DOMINANT RESULT - the session study. The four benchmark badges
		     that used to sit under the hero are here beside it, because they are
		     four DIFFERENT measurements: the ceiling is one corpus, the overall
		     is the whole standard corpus, the latency is the engine's own
		     overhead, the saved total is the study. A reader must not be able to
		     average them into one outcome, so every figure keeps the qualifier
		     that names its domain - and the qualifier is part of the number. -->
		<!-- BAND 04 // EVIDENCE - the inverted sand band: bone ground, carbon ink,
		     the page's only surface change. It is a BAND and not an inset pod
		     because a pod would have to be laid on the same dark ground as the
		     other five chapters, and the subject change would stop being visible
		     without a word; the pod's composition (one dominant figure, the
		     comparison beside it, the qualifier under it) is the band's own.
		     `border-t-0` because the bone surface IS the divider: the shell's grey
		     chapter rule would otherwise paint a 1px dark seam across the top edge
		     of the paper, which is the one place on the page it cannot sit. -->
		<section
			class="paper-band relative border-t-0 bg-bone-newsprint py-6 text-soot-black sm:py-10 lg:py-14"
			data-reveal
		>
			<!-- ONE EXPRESSIVE DEVICE IN THIS BAND: the giant numeral. The ribbon
			     that used to stamp the section is gone - a tape label AND a
			     kicker are two things introducing one section, which is the same
			     defect the mechanism band already answered by demoting its tape
			     to a kicker. The tape's one fact (how many sessions the study
			     covers) moves into the kicker, so nothing is lost but a second
			     device. The four chips below are flat for the same reason: with
			     the numeral as the band's one expressive assembly, a shadowed,
			     tilted chip is a second and third. -->
			<p class="section-index !mb-4 !text-oxblood-dark">
				04 // EVIDENCE // {Study.Sessions} SESSIONS
			</p>
			<h2 class="type-section mb-8 text-soot-black">
				One result, and the four figures beside it
			</h2>
			<div class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-y-2 border-soot-black py-8 lg:flex-row lg:items-end lg:gap-12">
				<div class="font-headline text-[clamp(3.5rem,11vw,8rem)] font-black leading-[0.85] tracking-tighter text-raw-blood">
					{Study.SavedPct}
				</div>
				<div class="max-w-xl">
					<p class="type-meta font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-oxblood-dark">
						CONTEXT TOKENS DEFLECTED
					</p>
					<p class="type-body mt-2 text-soot-black">
						Measured over {Study.Sessions} real Hermes sessions: {Study.SavedPct} of all
						context tokens deflected, a median session saved ratio of{" "}
						{Study.MedianRatio}, and ~{Study.AvgPerCall} tokens saved per API call.{" "}
						{Study.SessionsSaved} of the {Study.Sessions} sessions saved tokens.
					</p>
					<p class="type-body mt-3 text-oxblood-dark">
						The sensitivity band is {Study.Band}: the estimate depends on the
						chars-per-token ratio, which the study puts at {Study.CharsPerTok}.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="type-meta mt-10 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-oxblood-dark">
				THE OTHER FOUR MEASUREMENTS
			</p>
			<p class="type-body mt-2 max-w-[62ch] text-soot-black">
				Each of these is a different measurement in a different domain, and the qualifier
				under each one is part of the number. The ceiling is not the average, the average is
				not the latency, and the saved total is the session study rather than any single
				run.
			</p>
			<!-- BENTO, ONE DOMINANT + THREE SUPPORTING: the ceiling is the page's
			     headline figure, so it takes 1.5fr of a weighted track set while
			     the other three take 1fr each - a two-size cluster instead of a
			     row of four equals.

			     FOUR ACCENTS, AND THEY ARE IDENTITY, NOT DECORATION. These four
			     badges are four measurements in four different domains: the
			     ceiling is one corpus, the overall is the whole standard corpus,
			     the latency is the engine's own overhead, the saved total is the
			     session study. Their colours are the only cue saying the numbers
			     are not comparable, so each keeps its own surface and ink:

			       610x COMPRESSION      raw blood on bone white
			       132x OVERALL          carbon void, weathered teal ink
			       ~10MS E2E OVERHEAD    raw blood on bone white
			       156M+ TOKENS SAVED    stamped canary on soot black

			     "One accent per cluster" governs DECORATION - tape, rules,
			     shadows, chips that carry no meaning. It does not apply where
			     colour IS the distinction: flattening these four to one accent
			     was tried and the owner's verdict was that they "lost their
			     visual identity". Same carve-out as the four stage cards.
			     Emphasis is still the quiet tier: no shadow, no tilt, because
			     the giant numeral above is the band's one expressive assembly. -->
			<div
				class="mt-8 grid max-w-5xl grid-cols-1 items-start justify-items-center gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-[1.5fr_1fr_1fr_1fr]"
				data-enter="260"
			>
				<div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
					<span class="border-4 border-black bg-raw-blood px-6 py-3 font-mono text-lg font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-bone-white">
						<span data-count={Benchmark.Ceiling.replace("×", "")}>
							{Benchmark.Ceiling.replace("×", "")}
						</span>
						&times; COMPRESSION
					</span>
					<span class="max-w-[18rem] text-pretty text-base leading-relaxed text-oxblood-dark">
						Best case, on large low-entropy prose.
					</span>
				</div>
				<div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
					<span class="border-2 border-black bg-carbon-void px-4 py-1.5 font-mono text-base font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-weathered-teal">
						<span data-count={Benchmark.Overall.replace("×", "")}>
							{Benchmark.Overall.replace("×", "")}
						</span>
						&times; OVERALL
					</span>
					<span class="max-w-[15rem] text-pretty text-base leading-relaxed text-oxblood-dark">
						The whole standard corpus: {Benchmark.CorpusIn} to {Benchmark.CorpusOut}.
					</span>
				</div>
				<div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-2">
					<span class="border-2 border-black bg-raw-blood px-4 py-1.5 font-mono text-base font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-bone-white">
