						</div>
					))}
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="mt-16 grid grid-cols-1 gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-16">
				<article class="flex flex-col border-t-2 border-oxblood-dark pt-6">
					<p class="type-meta font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-zinc-dust">
						PATH 01 // NATIVE PLUGIN
					</p>
					<h3 class="type-sub mt-3 text-bone-white">Hermes plugin</h3>
					<div class="prose-flow mt-4 mb-6">
						<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
							Aphrodite connects to Hermes Agent as a native plugin, not as a proxy
							you point a client at. It is the reason the interception lives inside
							the turn: tool output never hits a wire, so only hook-level registration
							can catch it before it becomes message history - six hooks, thirteen
							tools, and an optional context engine, every call forwarded into the
							Rust dylib.
						</p>
						<p class="type-body text-bone-newsprint">
							The trade-off is stated plainly: the hooks are Hermes' contract - no
							other client can register them, so this lane works with Hermes only.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 border-t border-oxblood-dark pt-4">
						<a
							class="type-code font-bold text-stamped-canary hover:underline"
							href={Routes.Hermes}
